  "final_response": "1. **Final next action** — **request repair**.  \n   The retrieved evidence reveals a blocking nonconformity with the agreed intent, so acceptance is not appropriate.\n\n2. **Final implementation confidence** — **5 / 100**.  \n   A baseline implementation and some retry/test activity exist, but the authoritative-source requirement and required validation coverage are not satisfied.\n\n3. **Final acceptance-evidence sufficiency** — **insufficient now** for acceptance.  \n   It is sufficient to establish a blocking defect: the reconciliation proceeded after final HTTP 429 using a previous-night local snapshot for page 2, which conflicts with the agreement that local snapshots are for retry/resume, not silent authority substitution. The test list also lacks the required multi-subscription same-customer case.\n\n4. **Which retrieved evidence changed or failed to change the judgment, and why**  \n   - **SH2-R1 — changed judgment downward.**  \n     Observed artifact: page 1 returned HTTP 200; page 2 attempts 1–3 returned HTTP 429; after the final 429, reconciliation continued using **37 records from the previous-night local snapshot for page 2**.  \n     This is not demonstrated as mere retry/resume. It shows reconciliation continuing with stale local data after the authoritative Billing API failed, directly undermining billing API authority and risking incorrect entitlement/billing-period reconciliation.  \n   - **SH2-R4 — changed judgment downward.**  \n     Observed artifact: current tests cover successful single-subscription reconciliation, retry handling for HTTP 429, and timestamp conversion. But every customer in current tests has one subscription, and no test has two subscriptions for the same customer on the same report date.  \n     The agreed validation explicitly requires a multi-subscription same-customer case. That required acceptance coverage is absent. The retry test also does not prove idempotency or show that stale snapshots are not